# Sentinel Shield — Semgrep scope for Node / TypeScript service projects.
# Copy to your repository ROOT as `.semgrepignore`. The Sentinel Shield workflows
# run Semgrep with `-w /src` so this file (at the repo root) is honored.
#
# SCOPE: SEMGREP / SAST ONLY. It does NOT change:
#   npm audit · Trivy (fs/deps) · Syft SBOM · Gitleaks · OSV · Grype
# Those still scan dependencies, lockfiles, and the whole tree.
# (See docs/semgrep-scoping.md.)
#
# Goal: scan application source (src/, lib/, app/, server/); do NOT treat installed
# dependencies, build output or generated clients as application code.

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build / bundler / transpiler output
dist/
build/
out/
lib-cov/
coverage/
.next/
.turbo/
.cache/

# Generated clients / schemas (regenerate, do not hand-edit)
generated/
**/*.generated.ts
**/*.generated.js

# The embedded Sentinel Shield checkout is TOOLING, not application code. The managed
# workflows check the engine out here (SENTINEL_SHIELD_PATH) and run Semgrep from the
# repository root, so without this exclusion Semgrep analyses the engine's own scripts,
# examples and INTENTIONALLY INSECURE test fixtures as if they were your application —
# inflating findings, scan time and report size, and making your gate depend on which
# Sentinel Shield version you pinned. Keep this in sync with SENTINEL_SHIELD_PATH.
tools/sentinel-shield/

# Do NOT exclude your application source — keep src/, lib/, app/ and server/ scanned.
